WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Imagine a corporate
database with

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.139
information about
hundreds of employees.

3
00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:06.090
How can you calculate
important things

4
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.710
such as salary increases or

5
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.510
calculate changes
to allowances for

6
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.940
all the employees
accurately and efficiently?

7
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.875
With SQL you can use

8
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:16.645
arithmetic operators to
make these adjustments.

9
00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:18.155
By the end of this video,

10
00:00:18.155 --> 00:00:19.610
you will understand
and be able to

11
00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.935
describe SQL
arithmetic operators

12
00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:24.470
and know how to use these
arithmetic operators

13
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.350
to perform functions in SQL.

14
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.100
But first, what exactly
are operators in SQL?

15
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.840
Operators are specific
words or characters that

16
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.420
help you to perform different
activities in a database.

17
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.010
They're like conjunctions or

18
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.470
connection words you'd use
to compose a sentence.

19
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.090
Or the operation keys used to

20
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.360
perform a sum in a calculator.

21
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.155
Why do you need to
know about operators?

22
00:00:46.155 --> 00:00:48.965
Well, when handling
data in a database,

23
00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:50.720
at some point, you'll
need to query and

24
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.985
manipulate data for
different purposes.

25
00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.880
SQL operators allow you
to manipulate data as

26
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:56.930
necessary to perform

27
00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.110
these different activities
in the database.

28
00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:00.860
For example, you can use

29
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.780
an arithmetic
operation to calculate

30
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.890
how many leave days
an employee has left.

31
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.320
Or you can compare whether

32
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.665
employees are meeting
company targets.

33
00:01:08.665 --> 00:01:11.555
There are various types
of operators in SQL,

34
00:01:11.555 --> 00:01:13.310
each with different functions.

35
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.550
Let's explore a few examples
of arithmetic operators.

36
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.890
Arithmetic operators
are commonly used in

37
00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.390
computer languages to perform

38
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.595
a calculation and
return the result.

39
00:01:22.595 --> 00:01:25.655
Much like common arithmetic
operators in mathematics,

40
00:01:25.655 --> 00:01:28.090
you can use arithmetic
operators in SQL to

41
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.105
carry out mathematical
operations in a database.

42
00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:33.380
The SQL arithmetic operators and

43
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.095
their symbols are
plus for addition,

44
00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.395
subtraction and asterisk
for multiplication.

45
00:01:39.395 --> 00:01:41.225
Forward slash for division,

46
00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:43.580
percentage for modulus,
which provides

47
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.375
the remainder value of
a division calculation.

48
00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:49.650
How does SQL arithmetic
operations work?

49
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.735
When performing a calculation,

50
00:01:51.735 --> 00:01:55.090
an operator takes two operands
and returns a result.

51
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.595
For example, an addition
operator can take five

52
00:01:58.595 --> 00:02:02.315
as both of its operands and
returned ten as its result.

53
00:02:02.315 --> 00:02:04.955
In SQL, you can apply
the same concept

54
00:02:04.955 --> 00:02:07.870
by using the select command
for the various operations.

55
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.605
Let's illustrate this concept

56
00:02:09.605 --> 00:02:11.210
using the addition operator.

57
00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.390
You can use the select command
followed by one operand,

58
00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.030
the addition operator,
and the second operand.

59
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.875
Just like the previous example,

60
00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:22.400
SQL calculates the two operands
and produces the result.

61
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.350
You can repeat the SQL syntax

62
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.195
with the other
arithmetic operators.

63
00:02:26.195 --> 00:02:27.970
With the subtraction operator,

64
00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.870
the output result is 0.

65
00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.245
A multiplication operator
returns a result of 25.

66
00:02:33.245 --> 00:02:36.290
The division operator
calculates result as 1.

67
00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.940
With the modulus operator.

68
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.130
The result is 0 as 5 divided

69
00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.940
by 5 equals 1 with no remainder.

70
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.635
Now, let's take a closer look

71
00:02:45.635 --> 00:02:48.080
at how to use these
arithmetic operators in SQL.

72
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.390
I'll demonstrate how to use

73
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.310
arithmetic operators in SQL to

74
00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.815
perform basic
mathematical operations.

75
00:02:54.815 --> 00:02:57.835
Let's try an addition
operation to add two numbers.

76
00:02:57.835 --> 00:02:59.300
First, I use

77
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.405
the select command and then
type the numbers 10,15,

78
00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:04.325
separated by a plus operator,

79
00:03:04.325 --> 00:03:05.990
followed by a semicolon.

80
00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.795
Although the semicolon is
optional in this case,

81
00:03:08.795 --> 00:03:10.340
I prefer to still use it as it

82
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.925
represents the end
of a SQL statement.

83
00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:15.230
The select command
retrieves the value

84
00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.255
which is the sum of 10 plus 15,

85
00:03:17.255 --> 00:03:20.255
and displays it on screen.
Let's run this query.

86
00:03:20.255 --> 00:03:22.160
The query produces the result of

87
00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.060
the example addition
operation, which is 25.

88
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.800
Just as I perform this
addition operation,

89
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.915
I can do the same
with subtraction,

90
00:03:29.915 --> 00:03:33.985
multiplication, division,
and modulus operators.

91
00:03:33.985 --> 00:03:36.440
I can use the minus
sign for subtraction,

92
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.615
an asterisk star
for multiplication,

93
00:03:38.615 --> 00:03:40.325
forward slash for division,

94
00:03:40.325 --> 00:03:42.500
and the percentage
sign for modulus.

95
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.955
For example, I can type
select 100 modulus 10.

96
00:03:46.955 --> 00:03:49.310
This divides 100 by 10,

97
00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.225
and gives me the remainder
of the division operation.

98
00:03:52.225 --> 00:03:55.040
In this case, the
remainder is 0 as

99
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.550
100 divided by 10 equals
10 with no remainder.

100
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.410
I run the query and the
remainder of 0 is displayed.

101
00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.640
That's how you can use
the operator symbols for

102
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.135
different basic
operations in SQL.

103
00:04:07.135 --> 00:04:09.740
You've learned about secret
arithmetic operators

104
00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.890
and how to perform basic
operations with them in school.

105
00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.990
You're now ready to
learn how to apply

106
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.430
these arithmetic operators in

107
00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.770
more practical
ways. Awesome work.